{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/858745-another-attempt-at-simplified-combat","id":858745,"title":"Another attempt at simplified combat","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-04-03T20:58:50-07:00","id":858745,"title":"Another attempt at simplified combat","content":"This system tweak is inspired by the simplified combat rules in Changeling Storyteller's Guide, with the edge rules repurposed from the abandoned playtest of Fading Suns 3e (here named advantage). It allows for more detailed combat than the Down and Dirty CoD combat rules, using manoeuvres and creating dramatic tension, but is closer to the social combat system from Dead Man's Party and only requires one roll from each party. Like the Down and Dirty rules, the system is governed first and foremost by intent.<br /> <br /> In brief: state intent, make a contested roll between Attribute + Ability (difficulty as per list below), with three successes you achieve your intent.<br /> <br /> But, as always, there are modifiers and optional complications...<br /> <br /> Advantage: Instead of initiative, we use advantage. In short, everyone acts at once (all rolls are contested), but one party in every round will gain the advantage based on whether they initiated the combat or won the previous round. Advantage grants a single extra dice (or two if you did exceptionally well).<br /> <br /> So, the player who initiates combat gets an extra dice to roll on the first round. Whoever succeeds on the first round gets the extra dice on the second round. Etc. All rolls are otherwise considered to occur simultaneously. <br /> <br /> Physical Disciplines: Celerity, Potence, Fortitude or extra actions add extra dice to your roll (one each) instead of their usual effects. They may also affect the specifics of any advantage gained on 4+ successes, at the Storyteller's discretion (e.g., you successfully incapacitate your opponent but also manage to run clear of the scene too, preventing immediate reprisals). These bonuses do stack with advantage.<br /> <br /> Multiple opponents: If there are multiple attackers on one target, use the teamwork rules for determining gross successes. The defender deducts from this combined total as usual and the effect is determined by net successes. Obviously, ganging up on someone becomes pretty lethal.<br /> <br /> Defending: The contested rolls system implies a degree of weaving about and perhaps even parrying your opponent. But if the defender opts to spend their entire turn defending and nothing else (instead of actively fighting back), they can instead oppose each combatant with a fresh contested roll, but each combatant beyond the first adds +1 difficulty (if difficulty would exceed 9, add to successes threshold required). If defending, the target isn't required to score any net successes (merely reduce her opponent's successes to zero) to achieve her intent. A complete success (3 successes) grants him the advantage, however. An outstanding success (4+) might allow him to break combat, disarm his opponent or grab/break his opponent's weapon instead.<br /> <br /> Intent: Each opponent declares at the start what their intent for combat is. Prior to the first round, each player should say what their character hopes to achieve or avoid during combat, and then states their specific intent for each round (for example, which manoeuvre they're attempting or which body part they're aiming for). When each party declares their intent, they use the following difficulty table to resolve their actions (with some optional modifiers):<br /> Defend - difficulty 6<br /> Bruise (1-2 health levels) - difficulty 6<br /> Disarm - difficulty 7<br /> Stun (human) - difficulty 7<br /> Hurt (2-3 health levels) - difficulty 7<br /> Wound (3-4 health levels) - difficulty 8<br /> Stun (supernatural) - difficulty 8<br /> Pin - difficulty 8 (+/-1 if vast differences in weight)<br /> Escape - difficulty 8 (+1 if also pinned)<br /> Incapacitate - difficulty 9<br /> <br /> The rolls, as stated earlier, are always contested. The winner inflicts the full damage they intended or achieves their goal on three successes. Fewer than three successes and they succeed with a complication (you injure your foe, but not as gravely as you'd wished or you injure them but give them the advantage in the next turn). More than three successes and you succeed with some kind of additional or spectacular effect. A botch causes your opponent to win this particular round and you lose the opportunity to contest at all in the next round.<br /> <br /> Death: Killing first requires you to incapacitate your victim AND have the capacity to do so (for example, the ability to cause aggravated damage, or simply having the time to actually do so before being interrupted, etc). At the ST's discretion, if you incapacitate with 5+ net successes (if such a blow has the capacity to kill such a character ordinarily), you kill outright. Unless you specifically announced that you intended to fight to kill (e.g., you didn't just want answers or to escape or to show someone a lesson), killing an opponent first requires the expenditure of 1WP and probably calls for some kind of degeneration check. Optionally, character Natures may change or the ST may strike you down with a derangement if you fail a Willpower or virtue roll.<br /> <br /> Other modifiers: The ST may increase/decrease the difficulty of your intent if there's a big difference in the attacker's Strength/weapon damage and the defender's Stamina.<br /> <br /> Optional advanced modifiers: This rule allows for greater consideration of factors like Stamina and Strength and replaces the 'other modifiers' above.<br /> <br /> First, every player should calculate their aggregate scores in:<br /> Brawl (Dexterity + Brawl + base damage + Stamina)<br /> Melee (as above, but with Melee)<br /> Firearms (as above, but with Firearms)<br /> Thrown weapons (as above, but with Athletics)<br /> Dodge (Dexterity + Athletics + Wits + Stamina)<br /> <br /> These should be noted on the character sheet so they don't have to be repeatedly recalculated. Armour can also be factored in, if relevant, but not Potence, Celerity and Fortitude (as these already add extra dice).<br /> <br /> A character may spend 1WP to add their Wits (offensive actions only) OR Athletics (offensive close combat only) to their aggregate score for a single round of combat. This indicates quick thinking and darting about while attacking. Defending doesn't get this benefit, but 1WP spent allows you to break combat if you reduce your opponent's successes to zero, even if you don't score any net successes.<br /> <br /> At the start of combat, compare opponents' aggregate scores. The player with the higher score subtracts half the difference between the two from their own difficulty when rolling for intent, rounded down (min. difficulty 3). This allows stronger or tougher characters to more easily overpower or resist their foes. Note that average characters will be significantly disadvantaged against killing machines in this system (perhaps as it should be). Someone with two dots in each of their four traits (aggregate score: 8) will roll to incapacitate at difficulty 9, while their opponent with five dots in each of their four traits (aggregate score: 20) would roll difficulty 3 to do the same thing. Someone with all fours would roll at difficulty 5. Someone with all threes would roll at difficulty 7.<br /> <br /> Any thoughts or suggestions? I've been mulling this one over for a while.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":". 04-10-2016, 06:34 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-04T04:58:35-07:00","id":858897,"title":"","content":"One big problem I have with this (and all rules of this ilk) is there's no way for a combat to result in something that wasn't the intent of any of the participants. In my games, unwanted escalation and unintended outcomes have been the rule more than the exception when violence is employed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2082-schleiermacher","name":"Schleiermacher","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"190"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T13:39:23-07:00","id":859845,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Schleiermacher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/858745-another-attempt-at-simplified-combat?p=858897#post858897\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One big problem I have with this (and all rules of this ilk) is there's no way for a combat to result in something that wasn't the intent of any of the participants. In my games, unwanted escalation and unintended outcomes have been the rule more than the exception when violence is employed.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, yes there is, actually. If you roll fewer than three successes, you only get a partial success. That results in some kind of complication or unexpected result.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T13:58:09-07:00","id":859851,"title":"","content":"That's really not what I got from your examples of complications, but if you say so...<br /> <br /> And that has the opposite problem where people with 2-6 dice in combat, which should be most people, will rarely if ever get a result which doesn't introduce some kind of complication.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2082,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2082-schleiermacher","user":"Schleiermacher","date":". 04-05-2016, 05:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2082-schleiermacher","name":"Schleiermacher","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"190"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-05T14:04:39-07:00","id":859857,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Schleiermacher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/858745-another-attempt-at-simplified-combat?p=859851#post859851\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That's really not what I got from your examples of complications, but if you say so...<br /> <br /> And that has the opposite problem where people with 2-6 dice in combat, which should be most people, will rarely if ever get a result which doesn't introduce some kind of complication.</div> </div> </div> </div>Which, I guess, is more realistic. Currently, people still announce what they want to do, and the dice and ST adjudicate. This system only really reduces the number of rolls.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-08T20:04:02-07:00","id":862378,"title":"","content":"A friend of mine came up with a real quick and dirty system, add the attribute and ability+weapon bonus together then add that to a d10 roll. Do the same with the other person in the combat, the difference in rolls is how much damage they have to soak.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3953-dwight","name":"Dwight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3953_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T15:27:22-07:00","id":863349,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dwight</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/858745-another-attempt-at-simplified-combat?p=862378#post862378\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A friend of mine came up with a real quick and dirty system, add the attribute and ability+weapon bonus together then add that to a d10 roll. Do the same with the other person in the combat, the difference in rolls is how much damage they have to soak.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's not a bad idea. It still requires two rolls (attack and soak), but it's definitely less math and thinking time.<br /> <br /> Is weapon bonus including Strength? I'm assuming it is, because then the formula works for both close combat and ranged combat.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":5291,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","user":"adambeyoncelowe","date":". 04-10-2016, 06:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T15:37:27-07:00","id":863357,"title":"","content":"Yes it does like a sword is strength +2 or a heavy revolver is simply +6.<br /> <br /> If you wanted to just make it one dice roll just subtract the other person's soak pool(or half the soak pool) from the total dice roll and just have that be damage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3953-dwight","name":"Dwight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3953_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2355"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T09:48:04-07:00","id":867623,"title":"","content":"Admittedly I have not played much new edition and might not know something about the rules, but you're really letting people &quot;Incapacitate&quot; in a single roll? Why are there different levels of Bruise, Hurt, etc. if you removed the need to accumulate damage to eventually incapacitate or kill and opponent? The difficulty increase does not seem to be a sufficient downside compared to the increase in results you get.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2330-vree","name":"Vree","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"176"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}